ould be a good idea but good luck with  the project yeah and I'd add one more  thing the API supports both synchronous  and asynchronous audio so if you want to  do something like a Google assistant  scenario where it's like live happening  right in the moment you can use the  synchronous API or you can use the  asynchronous API for larger chunks of  audio pre-recorded audio that you're  trying to transcribe so it works both  ways all right next up is what are the  resource requirements to run tensorflow  at the edge in a scenario like this on  an edge gateway the way I'd answer that  is I think the pure number people think  about is oh I can measure flops doesn't  really work that way because model  complexity drives and the model type  itself drives a lot of your resource  requirements so what I would say is  figure out the problem you're trying to  solve add a sort of small medium large  budget of like hey I have I'm running a  big server at the edge versus as I'm  running a low power device like start  from that that sort of gives you a  constraint on am i doing hundreds of  thousands millions or hundreds of  millions of neurons right you're  starting to think like that and then  from there benchmark and iterate and  what you'll find is that the the if you  if you had done a flop space measurement  it doesn't scale linearly  because as your as your compute at the  edge goes down different bottlenecks  show up like memory bandwidth and things  like that that would put that impact how  well the the model runs and then the  other side of the equation is also think  about if you don't need your inference  to happen in 30 milliseconds then that  really frees you up in terms of how much  compute you need at the edge we've we  have scenarios where we've deployed  effectively a Raspberry Pi and you run  super slow but it was good enough for  that scenario right six seconds to do  inference but that was good enough for  what we were doing so think of it that  way and the